# Target C: nine-no-root k=6 depth-two synchronization

Date: 2026-10-03
Base main: 7a327e1ad95cd2e9cb035b9b96fb34fd8f12114d
Status: proof-only reduction. C-II and Level I remain open.

Let B have nine no-root rows and k=6. Current main gives D in {-2,-1},
rank(P)=3, and for every nonempty deleted coordinate set S,

    D(B-S)=D(B)+2|S|-3 q_S >= 0,

where q_S is the puncture rank loss of the coefficient space P. Hence

    D=-2: (d1,d2,d3)(P)>=(3,4,6),
    D=-1: (d1,d2,d3)(P)>=(2,4,5).

The root identity is D=p+3 d_root-9. The six arithmetic branches are

    D=-2: (d_root,p)=(0,7),(1,4),(2,1),
    D=-1: (d_root,p)=(0,8),(1,5),(2,2).

The current-main eight-no-root rank-three low-p theorem
research/target_c_support7_global_segment_relative_eight_noroot_k5_low_p_containment_lift_closure_2026-09-23.md
excludes the p=1 and p=2 parent branches after one deletion. It also implies:

    D=-2,p=4: every one-row shadow retains p=4;
    D=-1,p=5: every one-row shadow retains p=5;
    D=-2,p=7: at least seven coordinates retain p=7;
    D=-1,p=8: at least seven coordinates retain p=8.

Call a coordinate p-good when its one-row shadow retains p.

## Pair-rank structure

For D=-2, d1(P)>=3, so every coordinate pair preserves rank(P)=3.

For D=-1, distinct weight-two projective lines of P have disjoint supports.
Otherwise two independent weight-two words would span a two-dimensional
subcode on at most three coordinates, contradicting d2(P)>=4. Since rank(P)=3,
there are at most three such lines. Thus at least 33 of the 36 coordinate pairs
preserve rank.

For any rank-preserving pair {i,j}, the seven-row shadow has rank three, k=4,
and D_ij=D+4. Comparing root identities gives

    p-p_ij = 3(d_ij-d_root).                              (1)

So pair loss is a multiple of three.

## Pair-loss-three lemma

Assume i and j are individually p-good and deleting {i,j} loses exactly three
selected incomplete root rows. Every lost block then has exact external support
{i,j}. An incomplete root block contains at most two selected rows, so there
are at least two distinct lost blocks and at least one lost singleton block.

The current-main local four-cycle geometry from
research/target_c_support7_global_segment_relative_seven_noroot_k4_dminus2_d0p3_closure_2026-09-20.md
says that two no-root cycles jointly touching two distinct root blocks are two
different orientations of one four-set, and their local quotient directions
differ at each common root block.

At the lost singleton block, its singleton coefficient constraint is therefore
nonzero and supported inside {i,j}. This constraint lies in P. Thus pair loss
three forces a nonzero word of P supported on {i,j}.

Consequences:

- for D=-2, pair loss three is impossible;
- for D=-1, pair loss three is impossible on every rank-preserving pair.

## Four branch consequences

1. D=-2, (d_root,p)=(1,4).

All nine coordinates are p-good and all 36 pairs preserve rank. Equation (1)
allows only loss 0 or 3, and loss 3 is impossible. Therefore every pair shadow is

    seven no-root rows, rank(P)=3, k=4, D=2, d_root=1, p=4.

In particular every selected incomplete block in the parent is touched by at
least three no-root rows.

2. D=-1, (d_root,p)=(1,5).

All nine coordinates are p-good. Every rank-preserving pair retains p=5, so at
least 33 pair shadows are exactly

    seven no-root rows, rank(P)=3, k=4, D=3, d_root=1, p=5.

Any p-loss pair must be a weight-two support. Weight-two supports are disjoint,
and each p-loss consumes three selected rows, so at most one pair can lose p.
Hence at least 35 of all 36 pair deletions retain p=5.

3. D=-2, (d_root,p)=(0,7).

There is a p-good set G of size at least seven. All pairs preserve rank.
For pairs inside G, losses can be 0,3,6. Loss 3 is impossible. If loss 6 occurs,
then no lost block can be a singleton, or the same local argument would create
a weight-at-most-two word in P. Thus a six-loss pair consists of exactly three
selected pair blocks, all with exact support {i,j}; call this a triple-pair
packet. At most one such packet can occur because p=7. Therefore at least 20 of
the 21 pairs inside G retain p=7 and give exact seven-row (k,D,d_root,p)=(4,2,0,7)
shadows.

4. D=-1, (d_root,p)=(0,8).

There is a p-good set G of size at least seven. Among its 21 pairs, at least 18
preserve rank. On a rank-preserving pair, loss 3 is impossible, and loss 6 can
only be a triple-pair packet. At most one such packet can occur because p=8.
Therefore at least 17 pairs in G give exact rank-three seven-row
(k,D,d_root,p)=(4,3,0,8) shadows.

The next finite target is therefore the four seven-row positive-defect boundary
classes above, with simultaneous extension multiplicities 36, at least 33,
at least 20, and at least 17 respectively, rather than an unrestricted
nine-row k=6 search.

No numerical search, verifier output, floating-point calculation, or CI result
is used as a proof premise.


## Depth-three p=4 synchronization

In the D=-2, d_root=1, p=4 branch, every three-coordinate deletion also
retains p=4.

Let S={i,j,k}. The root-cost identity makes p-p_S a multiple of three, so a
loss would have size three. The depth-two result implies that every block lost
under deletion of S has exact external support S. Since an incomplete block
contains at most two selected root rows, total lost cost three uses at least
two distinct blocks and includes a singleton.

Thus i,j,k jointly touch two distinct root blocks. Current-main local
four-cycle geometry puts all three cycles on the same four-set, so they are its
three orientations and give the three distinct local projective directions at
the lost singleton block. The singleton constraint vanishes on the orientation
parallel to the selected root row and is nonzero on the other two. It is
therefore a weight-two nonzero word of the parent coefficient space P,
contradicting d1(P)>=3.

Hence all C(9,3)=84 triple deletions retain p=4, and every selected incomplete
block is touched by at least four of the nine no-root rows.

For every triple,

    D(B-S)=4-3q_S>=0,

so q_S is 0 or 1. The only six-row shadow types are

    q_S=0: rank(P_S)=3, k=3, D=4, d_root=1, p=4;
    q_S=1: rank(P_S)=2, k=4, D=1, d_root=1, p=4.

At least twenty triples have q_S=0. Indeed Q^9/P has dimension six; choose six
coordinate classes forming a basis. Every triple among them is independent
modulo P, giving C(6,3)=20 rank-preserving punctures.

This is proof-only. The next finite target is a simultaneous-extension
classification of these two six-row shadow types.
